IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wk-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for Business Activities Affecting Workers (2025): the Overview's
-- list, the general principles for analyzing agreements, section 1 (wage-fixing and
-- no-poach agreements) and section 2 (franchise no-poach agreements).
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW (n.1 [pdf page 2]). Every result is a sentence of
-- the text, attributed and with its own modal ("may", "can", "may require"); a sentence
-- the text states flatly ("are illegal even if", 1 [pdf page 6]) is reported as what the
-- agencies state.
-- No result says that a business acted lawfully or unlawfully.
--
-- Reports are LISTS of the sentences that apply, in the text's order. EMPTY means that no
-- sentence of that section applies to the facts given; it is not a finding that the
-- conduct is lawful. NOTHING means the answer turns on a finding the caller has not made
-- (whether there is an agreement; whether the businesses compete for workers), or that
-- the text gives no answer (fork F23).

§ `Overview, general principles, and sections 1 and 2`

§§ `Overview: the list of agreements and practices`

DECLARE `What the Overview says of a listed practice` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may investigate certain types of agreements or business practices as potential violations of the antitrust laws`
    `item 1: such agreements may violate the antitrust laws and may expose companies and executives to criminal liability`
    `item 1: where appropriate, DOJ exercises its authority to bring felony criminal charges against companies and individuals who participate in these conspiracies`
    `item 2: such agreements may violate the antitrust laws`
    `item 2: no-poach and similar agreements are subject to antitrust scrutiny even if they are between a franchisor and a franchisee or among the franchisees of the same franchisor`
    `item 3: such exchanges may violate the antitrust laws`
    `item 3: exchanging such information with competitors may be illegal even if companies use a third party or intermediary, including a third party using an algorithm`
    `item 4: such employment agreements may violate the antitrust laws`
    `item 4: these include non-compete provisions that prevent workers from leaving their job to join a competing or potentially competing employer or to start a new business, or that require workers to pay a penalty upon leaving their job`
    `item 5: such conditions may violate the antitrust laws`
    `item 5: these include overly broad non-disclosure agreements, training repayment agreement provisions, non-solicitation agreements, and exit fee or liquidated damages provisions`
    `this list is not exhaustive, and listed activities may or may not be an antitrust violation`

-- Overview [pdf page 3]: "the Agencies may investigate certain types of agreements or
-- business practices as potential violations of the antitrust laws. Examples of such
-- agreements include:" items 1 to 5, each with its own modal, then "This list is not
-- exhaustive. Listed activities may or may not be an antitrust violation." A practice the
-- list does not name answers NOTHING: not being listed is not a statement about it (fork
-- F3). The caller names the item; the items' facts are read in sections 1 to 5 (fork F22).
@ref Overview, items 1-5 [pdf page 3]
GIVEN p IS A `Practice in the Overview's list`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What the Overview says of a listed practice`)
`Overview: what the list says of the practice` MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `item 1: an agreement between companies not to recruit, solicit, or hire workers, or to fix wages or terms of employment`
        THEN JUST (LIST investigate, `item 1: such agreements may violate the antitrust laws and may expose companies and executives to criminal liability`, `item 1: where appropriate, DOJ exercises its authority to bring felony criminal charges against companies and individuals who participate in these conspiracies`, notExhaustive)
    WHEN `item 2: an agreement in the franchise context not to poach, hire, or solicit employees of the franchisor or franchisees`
        THEN JUST (LIST investigate, `item 2: such agreements may violate the antitrust laws`, `item 2: no-poach and similar agreements are subject to antitrust scrutiny even if they are between a franchisor and a franchisee or among the franchisees of the same franchisor`, notExhaustive)
    WHEN `item 3: an exchange of competitively sensitive information with companies that compete for workers`
        THEN JUST (LIST investigate, `item 3: such exchanges may violate the antitrust laws`, `item 3: exchanging such information with competitors may be illegal even if companies use a third party or intermediary, including a third party using an algorithm`, notExhaustive)
    WHEN `item 4: an employment agreement that restricts workers' freedom to leave their job`
        THEN JUST (LIST investigate, `item 4: such employment agreements may violate the antitrust laws`, `item 4: these include non-compete provisions that prevent workers from leaving their job to join a competing or potentially competing employer or to start a new business, or that require workers to pay a penalty upon leaving their job`, notExhaustive)
    WHEN `item 5: another restrictive, exclusionary, or predatory employment condition that harms competition`
        THEN JUST (LIST investigate, `item 5: such conditions may violate the antitrust laws`, `item 5: these include overly broad non-disclosure agreements, training repayment agreement provisions, non-solicitation agreements, and exit fee or liquidated damages provisions`, notExhaustive)
    WHEN `an agreement or business practice the list does not name` THEN NOTHING
  WHERE
    investigate MEANS `the agencies may investigate certain types of agreements or business practices as potential violations of the antitrust laws`
    notExhaustive MEANS `this list is not exhaustive, and listed activities may or may not be an antitrust violation`

§§ `General principles: the evidence of an agreement`

DECLARE `Kind of evidence of an agreement` IS ONE OF
    `discussing with another company wages or other potential terms of employment`
    `parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding`
    `an invitation to another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan`
    `use of a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information`
    `other direct or circumstantial evidence`

-- General principles [pdf page 4]: "In assessing whether businesses have entered into an
-- illegal agreement, the Agencies consider direct and circumstantial evidence. For
-- example, they may consider whether a business has" done one of four things. A report of
-- the kinds present, in the text's order, with any other evidence the caller offers
-- reported once at the end (the list is an example: fork F3). Not a weighing, and not a
-- finding of an agreement: that is the caller's (fork F2).
@ref General principles [pdf page 4]
GIVEN e IS A `Evidence of an agreement`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Kind of evidence of an agreement`
`General principles: the kinds of evidence of an agreement that are present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF e's `a business has discussed with another company wages or other potential terms of employment` THEN JUST `discussing with another company wages or other potential terms of employment` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `a business has engaged in parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding` THEN JUST `parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `a business has invited another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan` THEN JUST `an invitation to another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `a business has used a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information` THEN JUST `use of a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF null (e's `other direct or circumstantial evidence offered`) THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST `other direct or circumstantial evidence`))

§§ `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers`

DECLARE `Whether the businesses compete for workers` IS ONE OF
    `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
    `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
    `the businesses do not compete for workers in any labor market named`

-- General principles [pdf page 4]: "the Agencies will focus on whether the businesses
-- compete in the same labor markets even if they do not compete as sellers of products or
-- services", and companies can be labor market competitors "even if they have some other
-- collaborative or cooperative relationship, such as a joint venture", or are not
-- competitors downstream ("airplane manufacturers and their part suppliers may both hire
-- from the same market for engineers").
-- Read as: they compete when both hire or retain workers in a labor market the caller
-- names the same way; the products and the collaborative relationship are not read (fork
-- F1). Failing that, the caller's finding that they are potentially competing employers
-- (1 [pdf page 6]). NOTHING when either business names no labor market and there is no
-- such finding: the caller has not said.
@ref General principles [pdf page 4]; 1 [pdf page 6]
GIVEN b IS A `Two businesses`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Whether the businesses compete for workers`
`General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF any (GIVEN m YIELD elem m theirs) mine THEN JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
      IF b's `the businesses are potentially competing employers` THEN JUST `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
      IF null mine OR null theirs THEN NOTHING
      OTHERWISE JUST `the businesses do not compete for workers in any labor market named`
  WHERE
    mine MEANS b's `one business`'s `the labor markets in which it hires or retains workers`
    theirs MEANS b's `the other business`'s `the labor markets in which it hires or retains workers`

§§ `Section 1: what the businesses agree, in substance`

DECLARE `What the agreement is, in substance (section 1)` IS ONE OF
    `a wage-fixing agreement`
    `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    `an agreement that may be a form of wage fixing`

-- 1 [pdf pages 5-6] and n.11 [pdf page 5]: "The Agencies focus on the substance of a
-- wage-fixing or no-poach agreement regardless of its precise form."
--   * wage-fixing: an agreement about salaries or other terms of compensation "either at a
--     specific level or within a range"; to "align, stabilize, or otherwise coordinate the
--     wages they set, including by agreeing to a range, ceiling, or benchmark", where "it
--     does not matter if they do not agree on a specific wage";
--   * "an agreement to set a starting point for compensation may be a form of wage
--     fixing under the law": the text's "may" is kept (fork F6);
--   * no-poach: not to hire, solicit, and/or otherwise compete for current, former, or
--     potential workers; a restriction "it does not matter if the agreement does not
--     completely prohibit"; an agreement not to "cold call" workers "is considered a
--     no-solicit agreement regardless of whether the firms are allowed to hire the
--     workers who applied" (the field is not read); n.11's agreement "to request
--     permission from the other company before trying to hire an employee".
-- n.11's agreement between an employer and its workers not to solicit clients or vendors
-- is not one of these: it is section 5's non-solicitation agreement, a different record.
-- Something else: NOTHING.
@ref 1 [pdf pages 5-6]; n.11 [pdf page 5]
GIVEN a IS A `What the businesses agree`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What the agreement is, in substance (section 1)`
`1: what the agreement is, in substance` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `to fix workers' salaries or other terms of compensation or employment, such as bonuses or benefits, at a specific level or within a range` THEN JUST `a wage-fixing agreement`
    WHEN `to align, stabilize, or otherwise coordinate the wages they set, as by a range, ceiling, or benchmark for calculating wages, without agreeing on a specific wage` THEN JUST `a wage-fixing agreement`
    WHEN `to set a starting point for compensation` THEN JUST `an agreement that may be a form of wage fixing`
    WHEN `not to recruit, solicit, hire, or otherwise compete for current, former, or potential workers` THEN JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    WHEN `to restrict a company's ability to hire another company's workers, without completely prohibiting it` THEN JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    WHEN `not to cold call workers` THEN JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    WHEN `to request permission from the other company before trying to hire an employee` THEN JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    WHEN `something else relating to workers` THEN NOTHING

-- The two kinds section 1's sentences are about ("these agreements"); the starting point,
-- which "may be a form of wage fixing", is not one of them (fork F6).
@ref 1 [pdf pages 5-6]
GIVEN a IS A `What the businesses agree`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1: the agreement is a wage-fixing or no-poach agreement` IF
       `1: what the agreement is, in substance` a EQUALS JUST `a wage-fixing agreement`
    OR `1: what the agreement is, in substance` a EQUALS JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`

§§ `Section 1: wage-fixing and no-poach agreements`

DECLARE `What section 1 says of the agreement` IS ONE OF
    `businesses that compete with each other for workers may be committing an antitrust crime if they enter into such an agreement`
    `even if criminal charges are not pursued, such an agreement may also be subject to civil liability`
    `the agencies state that such an agreement, formed between competing or potentially competing employers, directly or through an intermediary, is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages`
    `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects, the restraint being subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration and reasonably necessary to achieve its procompetitive potential`
    `an agreement to set a starting point for compensation may be a form of wage fixing under the law`
    `the DOJ may criminally investigate and, where appropriate, bring felony charges against the participants in such an agreement, including both individuals and companies`

-- 1 [pdf pages 5-6], in the text's order:
--   * "Businesses that compete with each other for workers may be committing an antitrust
--     crime if they enter into an agreement not to recruit, solicit, or hire workers or to
--     fix wages or terms of employment." Read for businesses that compete (fork F4).
--   * "Even if criminal charges are not pursued, these agreements may also be subject to
--     civil liability." The same agreements.
--   * "When formed between competing or potentially competing employers, these types of
--     agreements ... are illegal even if they did not result in actual harm such as lower
--     wages." Competing or potentially competing (fork F4); n.15 [pdf page 6]: "Such
--     agreements may require a fuller analysis of their effects, however, when the
--     restraint is subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration ... and
--     is reasonably necessary to achieve the procompetitive potential of that
--     collaboration." Both conjuncts, read from their own fields; n.15 then replaces this
--     sentence and no other (fork F5).
--   * The starting point "may be a form of wage fixing", between competing or potentially
--     competing employers (the same paragraph); the other sentences are not given for it
--     (fork F6).
--   * "The DOJ may criminally investigate and, where appropriate, bring felony charges
--     against the participants in these agreements": "these agreements" are the
--     paragraph's, between competing or potentially competing employers.
-- Not read: the form of the agreement, who made it, whether through an intermediary,
-- whether carried out, whether it did harm, whether hiring of applicants remains
-- allowed, and the kind of workers (6: workers include independent contractors; fork
-- F10). Guards: NOTHING when whether there is an agreement has not been determined, or
-- when whether the businesses compete for workers is not stated and the agreement is one
-- the sentences are about (wage fixing, no-poach, or the starting point); EMPTY when the
-- agencies find no agreement.
@ref 1 [pdf pages 5-6]; n.11 [pdf page 5]; n.15 [pdf page 6]; General principles [pdf pages 3-4]
GIVEN x IS A `Agreement between businesses relating to workers`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What section 1 says of the agreement`)
`1: what section 1 says of the agreement` MEANS
    CONSIDER x's `the finding on whether there is an agreement`
    WHEN `the agencies identify an agreement between the businesses` THEN
        IF competition EQUALS NOTHING AND (wageOrPoach OR startingPoint)
        THEN NOTHING
        ELSE JUST (catMaybes (LIST (IF wageOrPoach AND competing THEN JUST `businesses that compete with each other for workers may be committing an antitrust crime if they enter into such an agreement` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF wageOrPoach AND competing THEN JUST `even if criminal charges are not pursued, such an agreement may also be subject to civil liability` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF wageOrPoach AND competingOrPotential AND (NOT ancillary) THEN JUST `the agencies state that such an agreement, formed between competing or potentially competing employers, directly or through an intermediary, is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF wageOrPoach AND competingOrPotential AND ancillary THEN JUST `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects, the restraint being subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration and reasonably necessary to achieve its procompetitive potential` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF startingPoint AND competingOrPotential THEN JUST `an agreement to set a starting point for compensation may be a form of wage fixing under the law` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF wageOrPoach AND competingOrPotential THEN JUST `the DOJ may criminally investigate and, where appropriate, bring felony charges against the participants in such an agreement, including both individuals and companies` ELSE NOTHING)))
    WHEN `the agencies find no agreement between the businesses` THEN JUST EMPTY
    WHEN `whether there is an agreement has not been determined` THEN NOTHING
  WHERE
    competition MEANS `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (x's `the businesses`)
    competing MEANS competition EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
    competingOrPotential MEANS competing OR competition EQUALS JUST `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
    wageOrPoach MEANS `1: the agreement is a wage-fixing or no-poach agreement` (x's `what the businesses agree`)
    startingPoint MEANS `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `an agreement that may be a form of wage fixing`
    ancillary MEANS x's `the restraint is subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration, such as a joint venture` AND x's `the restraint is reasonably necessary to achieve the procompetitive potential of that collaboration`

DECLARE `What section 1 says of the report` IS ONE OF
    `the Criminal Antitrust Anti-Retaliation Act provides whistleblower protections for employees, contractors, subcontractors, and agents who report antitrust crimes, including no-poach and wage-fixing agreements`

-- 1 [pdf page 6], n.17 [pdf page 6]: the text's one sentence on the Criminal Antitrust
-- Anti-Retaliation Act, for the four roles it names reporting an antitrust crime (no-poach
-- and wage-fixing agreements included). Another role, or conduct that is not an antitrust
-- crime: NOTHING, since the text says nothing of them. The Act itself (15 U.S.C. 7a-3) is
-- a statute and is not encoded (fork F21).
@ref 1 [pdf page 6]; n.17 [pdf page 6]
GIVEN r IS A `Report of an antitrust crime`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 1 says of the report`
`1: what section 1 says of a report of an antitrust crime` MEANS
    IF     (NOT (r's `the role of the person reporting` EQUALS `another person`))
       AND (NOT (r's `what the person reports` EQUALS `conduct that is not an antitrust crime`))
    THEN JUST `the Criminal Antitrust Anti-Retaliation Act provides whistleblower protections for employees, contractors, subcontractors, and agents who report antitrust crimes, including no-poach and wage-fixing agreements`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Section 2: franchise no-poach agreements`

DECLARE `What section 2 says of the agreement` IS ONE OF
    `no-poach clauses in franchise agreements are subject to antitrust scrutiny`
    `the agencies state that such an agreement between a franchisor and its franchisee not to compete for workers can be per se illegal under the antitrust laws: the agreement itself may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers`
    `a franchisor may violate the antitrust laws by organizing or enforcing a no-poach agreement among franchisees that compete for workers`
    `agreements among franchisees, either written or unwritten, not to poach, hire, or solicit each other's workers may violate other federal and state laws`

-- 2 [pdf pages 6-7], for a no-poach agreement (section 1's substance):
--   * between a franchisor and its franchisee: "No-poach clauses in franchise agreements
--     are also subject to antitrust scrutiny", and "Such an agreement can be per se
--     illegal ... In other words, the agreement itself may be illegal regardless of whether
--     it actually harms workers". Not conditioned on their competing for workers ("Often,
--     franchisors compete with franchisees"): fork F7. Section 1's sentences apply as well
--     when they do compete (that is section 1's function).
--   * among franchisees: "a franchisor may violate the antitrust laws by organizing or
--     enforcing a no-poach agreement among franchisees that compete for workers" (fork F9),
--     and such agreements, "either written or unwritten ... may violate other federal and
--     state laws" (not the antitrust laws; n.21's state statutes are not encoded).
-- The Overview's "subject to antitrust scrutiny" for franchisees is the Overview's (item
-- 2). Guards: as section 1; and NOTHING for an agreement among franchisees that the
-- franchisor organizes or enforces when whether they compete for workers is not stated.
-- The kind of workers is not read.
@ref 2 [pdf pages 6-7]; n.18 [pdf page 6]
GIVEN x IS A `Agreement between businesses relating to workers`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What section 2 says of the agreement`)
`2: what section 2 says of the agreement` MEANS
    CONSIDER x's `the finding on whether there is an agreement`
    WHEN `the agencies identify an agreement between the businesses` THEN
        BRANCH
          IF (NOT noPoach) THEN JUST EMPTY
          IF relationship EQUALS `a franchisor and its franchisee`
            THEN JUST (LIST `no-poach clauses in franchise agreements are subject to antitrust scrutiny`, `the agencies state that such an agreement between a franchisor and its franchisee not to compete for workers can be per se illegal under the antitrust laws: the agreement itself may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers`)
          IF relationship EQUALS `franchisees of the same franchisor` AND organizes AND competition EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
          IF relationship EQUALS `franchisees of the same franchisor`
            THEN JUST (catMaybes (LIST (IF organizes AND competing THEN JUST `a franchisor may violate the antitrust laws by organizing or enforcing a no-poach agreement among franchisees that compete for workers` ELSE NOTHING),
                                       (JUST `agreements among franchisees, either written or unwritten, not to poach, hire, or solicit each other's workers may violate other federal and state laws`)))
          OTHERWISE JUST EMPTY
    WHEN `the agencies find no agreement between the businesses` THEN JUST EMPTY
    WHEN `whether there is an agreement has not been determined` THEN NOTHING
  WHERE
    relationship MEANS x's `the franchise relationship of the parties`
    organizes MEANS x's `the franchisor organizes or enforces the agreement`
    competition MEANS `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (x's `the businesses`)
    competing MEANS competition EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
    noPoach MEANS `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`

§§ `General principles: whether the agreement's effects matter`

-- 6 [pdf page 11], read here because the general principles' question needs it: "an
-- agreement between two or more competing platforms to fix the compensation of
-- independent contractors offering their services via the platforms". "Competing" is
-- read as competing for those workers in the same labor market, and "fix the
-- compensation" as section 1's wage-fixing substance (fork F11).
@ref 6 [pdf page 11]
GIVEN x IS A `Agreement between businesses relating to workers`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `6: the agreement is one between competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors` IF
       x's `the businesses are platforms through which the independent contractors offer their services`
   AND (NOT (x's `the workers concerned` EQUALS `employees`))
   AND `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `a wage-fixing agreement`
   AND `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (x's `the businesses`) EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`

DECLARE `What the text says of whether the agreement's effects matter` IS ONE OF
    `section 1: the agencies state that such an agreement is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages`
    `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects`
    `section 2: the agencies state that such an agreement can be per se illegal, and may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers`
    `section 6: such an agreement may constitute the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that exposes the platforms to criminal liability`

-- General principles [pdf page 4]: "If the Agencies identify an agreement between
-- companies relating to workers, they assess its impact on competition and the
-- competitive process. Some types of agreements are illegal regardless of their effects.
-- In other cases, the Agencies perform a deeper analysis". The text says which is which
-- only in sections 1, 2 and 6 and n.15, and this lists every one of those statements that
-- applies, each with its own section's modal, with no precedence among them (fork F20):
--   * section 1 [pdf page 6], competing or potentially competing employers, wage-fixing or no-poach:
--     "illegal even if they did not result in actual harm"; or, n.15's two conjuncts met,
--     "may require a fuller analysis of their effects" in its place (fork F5);
--   * section 2 [pdf pages 6-7], a franchisor and its franchisee, no-poach: "can be per se illegal";
--   * section 6 [pdf page 11], competing platforms fixing independent contractors' compensation: "may
--     constitute the type of per se violation".
-- Section 3's effect sentence is section 3's, because an exchange of information need not
-- be an agreement (3 [pdf page 7] "Sharing ... may violate"); it is not read here.
-- NOTHING when no agreement is identified (the sentence begins "If the Agencies identify
-- an agreement"), when whether the businesses compete for workers is not stated, and when
-- none of those statements applies: the text's "Some types" is open ("including",
-- 1's heading), so the text does not say which way another type goes (fork F3).
@ref General principles [pdf page 4]; 1 [pdf page 6]; n.15 [pdf page 6]; 2 [pdf pages 6-7]; 6 [pdf page 11]
GIVEN x IS A `Agreement between businesses relating to workers`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What the text says of whether the agreement's effects matter`)
`General principles: what the text says of whether the agreement's effects matter` MEANS
    IF     (NOT (x's `the finding on whether there is an agreement` EQUALS `the agencies identify an agreement between the businesses`))
        OR competition EQUALS NOTHING
        OR null statements
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST statements
  WHERE
    competition MEANS `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (x's `the businesses`)
    competingOrPotential MEANS    competition EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
                               OR competition EQUALS JUST `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
    wageOrPoach MEANS `1: the agreement is a wage-fixing or no-poach agreement` (x's `what the businesses agree`)
    noPoach MEANS `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `a no-poach agreement, also called a no-hire, no-solicit, or market allocation agreement`
    ancillary MEANS x's `the restraint is subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration, such as a joint venture` AND x's `the restraint is reasonably necessary to achieve the procompetitive potential of that collaboration`
    statements MEANS catMaybes (LIST (IF wageOrPoach AND competingOrPotential AND (NOT ancillary) THEN JUST `section 1: the agencies state that such an agreement is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages` ELSE NOTHING),
                                     (IF wageOrPoach AND competingOrPotential AND ancillary THEN JUST `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects` ELSE NOTHING),
                                     (IF noPoach AND x's `the franchise relationship of the parties` EQUALS `a franchisor and its franchisee` THEN JUST `section 2: the agencies state that such an agreement can be per se illegal, and may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers` ELSE NOTHING),
                                     (IF `6: the agreement is one between competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors` x THEN JUST `section 6: such an agreement may constitute the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that exposes the platforms to criminal liability` ELSE NOTHING))
